{"name":"Golden Blood","hex":"#ff1155","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"golden","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of, or relating to, gold.","Having a color or other richness suggestive of gold."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["(Can we verify this sense?) brassy drummer, brassy chub, etc. (Kyphosus vaigiensis), a fish found in southeast Asia.","Ellipsis of golden retriever."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/golden"},{"term":"blood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A vital liquid flowing in the bodies of many types of animals that usually conveys nutrients and oxygen. In vertebrates, it is colored red by hemoglobin, is conveyed by arteries and veins, is pumped by the heart and is usually generated in bone marrow.","A family relationship due to birth, such as that between siblings; contrasted with relationships due to marriage or adoption. See blood relative, blood relation."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cause something to be covered with blood; to bloody.","To let blood (from); to bleed."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blood"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rh blood group system","blocks":[{"text":"The Rh blood group system is a human blood group system. It contains proteins on the surface of red blood cells. After the ABO blood group system, it is most likely to be involved in transfusion reactions. The Rh blood group system consists of over 50 defined blood group antigens, of which the five antigens D, C, c, E, and e are among the most prominent. There is no d antigen. Rh(D) status of an individual is normally described with a positive (+) or negative (−) suffix after the ABO type. The terms Rh factor, Rh positive, and Rh negative refer to the Rh(D) antigen only. Antibodies to Rh antigens can be involved in hemolytic transfusion reactions and antibodies to the Rh(D) and Rh antigens confer significant risk of hemolytic disease of the newborn."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Bonnet_macaque%2C_India%2C_2013-04-28.jpg/330px-Bonnet_macaque%2C_India%2C_2013-04-28.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rh_blood_group_system","wikidata":"Q425832"},{"title":"Blood","blocks":[{"text":"Blood is a specialized form of connective tissue and body fluid in the circulatory system of humans and other vertebrates that delivers necessary substances such as nutrients and oxygen to the cells of the body, and transports metabolic waste products away from those same cells."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Venous_and_arterial_blood.jpg/330px-Venous_and_arterial_blood.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blood","wikidata":"Q7873"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Bonnet_macaque%2C_India%2C_2013-04-28.jpg/500px-Bonnet_macaque%2C_India%2C_2013-04-28.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Bonnet_macaque%2C_India%2C_2013-04-28.jpg/1280px-Bonnet_macaque%2C_India%2C_2013-04-28.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Bonnet_macaque%2C_India%2C_2013-04-28.jpg/500px-Bonnet_macaque%2C_India%2C_2013-04-28.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The name rhesus factor (Rh) goes back to the use of erythrocytes extracted from the blood of rhesus monkeys for obtaining the first blood serum.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARhesus_monkey.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Rh_positive_vs_negative.png/500px-Rh_positive_vs_negative.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Rh_positive_vs_negative.png/1280px-Rh_positive_vs_negative.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Rh_positive_vs_negative.png/500px-Rh_positive_vs_negative.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rh-positive individuals have red blood cells with the Rh antigen (left) while Rh-negative individuals do not but will have antibodies against the antigen (right).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARh_positive_vs_negative.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/1GZX_Haemoglobin.png/500px-1GZX_Haemoglobin.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/1GZX_Haemoglobin.png/1280px-1GZX_Haemoglobin.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/1GZX_Haemoglobin.png/500px-1GZX_Haemoglobin.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hemoglobin, a globular proteingreen = haem (or heme) groupsred & blue = protein subunits","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1GZX_Haemoglobin.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Krew_Frakcjonowana.jpg/120px-Krew_Frakcjonowana.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Krew_Frakcjonowana.jpg/250px-Krew_Frakc